  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Partition dataset indices into K contiguous test folds and complementary training folds.\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = \\bigcup_{k=1}^K \\mathcal{D}_k, \\quad \\mathcal{D}_i \\cap \\mathcal{D}_j = \\emptyset, \\quad \\mathcal{D}_{\\text{train}}^{(k)} = \\mathcal{D} \\setminus \\mathcal{D}_k\n$$\n\nGiven input parameters `n_samples`, `n_splits`, return list of dictionaries containing 'train' and 'test' integer index lists.",
  "theory": "## What Is K-Fold Cross-Validation?\n\nIn classical machine learning, algorithms extract predictive decision boundaries, cluster structures, or low-dimensional manifolds from structured feature tables.\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = \\bigcup_{k=1}^K \\mathcal{D}_k, \\quad \\mathcal{D}_i \\cap \\mathcal{D}_j = \\emptyset, \\quad \\mathcal{D}_{\\text{train}}^{(k)} = \\mathcal{D} \\setminus \\mathcal{D}_k\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulation & Theoretical Mechanics\n\nSupervised and unsupervised learning algorithms minimize empirical risk over a hypothesis space $\\mathcal{H}$ while regularizing model capacity to balance bias and variance on unseen test distributions.\n\nThe full mathematical structure is governed by the coupled relations:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = \\bigcup_{k=1}^K \\mathcal{D}_k\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}_i \\cap \\mathcal{D}_j = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{train}}^{(k)} = \\mathcal{D} \\setminus \\mathcal{D}_k\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Key Properties & Conceptual Intuition\n\n- **Inductive Bias & Capacity Control:** Regularization penalties ($L_1$, $L_2$, pruning, or ensemble averaging) constrain effective hypothesis complexity and prevent overfitting to training noise.\n- **Decision Boundary Geometry:** Linear models partition feature space with hyperplanes, distance-based methods induce Voronoi tessellations, and tree ensembles form axis-aligned orthotopes.\n- **Related Topics in This Track:** **Leaky ReLU**, **ReLU**, **Sigmoid**.\n\n---\n\n## Numerical Stability & Boundary Behavior\n\n- **Conditioning & Degenerate Regimes:** When denominators, norms, or variances approach zero on constant or collinear inputs, additive regularization ($\\varepsilon > 0$) or limiting analytical values prevent division-by-zero instabilities.\n- **Scale & Precision Invariance:** Formulating updates via centered shifts (such as log-sum-exp shifting for exponentials or Welford-style centering for moments) avoids catastrophic cancellation and floating-point overflow.\n\n---\n\n## Computational Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ is the total number of input elements, executing in a single vectorized pass.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to hold the output array (or $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ auxiliary working memory beyond the result).",
